{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' advisor-approach.txt\"","aggregated_output":"Do not proceed unchanged. The specification curve is sensible, but trimming creates a material estimand defect.\n\nUntrimmed estimates target the ATT for all 185 NSW treated units. Trimming away treated units outside CPS support instead targets an overlap-subpopulation ATT. Its gap from the full-sample experimental benchmark cannot be interpreted as matching bias or “recovery”; treatment effects may differ in the retained subgroup. Separate trimmed results as a different-estimand sensitivity analysis, or construct specification-specific experimental overlap benchmarks by applying the same pre-treatment score rule to both NSW arms. Do not treat every cell as estimating one common effect.\n\nAlso correct these details:\n\n- If the score is refit after trimming, recheck overlap using the refitted score. The initial intersection no longer guarantees final-score support.\n- Form ATT subclasses from treated-score quintiles, not pooled-sample quintiles—the 15,992 CPS controls would dominate pooled cutpoints. Require treated and controls in every stratum, merge empty/sparse adjacent strata, and report balance and counts.\n- CR1 clustering by reused control is only a conditional-on-the-realized-match sandwich calculation. It captures control reuse but not propensity-score estimation, support selection, or nearest-neighbor matching discrepancy; a \\(t\\) critical value over unique controls does not repair that. Use an Abadie–Imbens-style analytic variance appropriate to estimated-score matching, or explicitly label the CR1 intervals as conditional/descriptive rather than full 95% sampling intervals.\n- The stratification plug-in variance likewise conditions on estimated scores and cutpoints. Prefer a full bootstrap that refits the score and reconstructs strata for subclassification only, or an appropriate stacked sandwich estimator.\n- Do not infer “recovery” from overlapping intervals. Ideally report uncertainty for each gap from the randomized benchmark, accounting for the shared NSW treated outcomes.\n\nProceed after those corrections. Otherwise favorable trimmed estimates could appear to recover the benchmark simply because the treated population changed, while the intervals overstate their inferential defensibility.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' terra-peer.txt\"","aggregated_output":"Mostly, but two issues are material.\n\n1. Trimming changes the estimand. Dropping treated units outside CPS support no longer targets the ATT for all NSW treated units; it targets the overlap-treated ATT. Do not compare that estimate as if it were directly interchangeable with the full experimental treated–control benchmark.\n\nConcrete correction: define support using the score from the initial composite-sample fit, retain treated and controls in the score-range intersection, and label trimmed rows “overlap-treated ATT.” Either retain that initial score for estimation, or—if refitting after trimming—recheck and report overlap under the refitted score. For an apples-to-apples causal benchmark of the trimmed target, use an experimental comparison adjusted/reweighted to the retained treated covariate distribution; otherwise treat comparison with the full NSW benchmark as descriptive only.\n\n2. The proposed 1-NN interval is not a generally valid matching interval. Clustering matched-pair differences by reused control captures one dependency source, but it does not provide the Abadie–Imbens variance correction for nearest-neighbor matching, including generated-score and nonsmooth-match uncertainty. “No ordinary bootstrap” is right; CR1 is not an adequate replacement.\n\nConcrete correction: use an Abadie–Imbens analytic variance estimator for matching with replacement (including reuse counts and conditional outcome-variance estimation, with the estimated-propensity-score correction where applicable). Report the number of distinct matched controls and maximum/typical score distance as diagnostics.\n\nThe point estimators are otherwise reasonable, with two clarifications:\n\n- 1-NN with replacement estimates the mean treated-minus-matched-control contrast for the retained treated set; without trimming it is formally computable but can rest on poor overlap. Report balance and match distances.\n- Treated-quintile subclassification with treated-share weights is the appropriate ATT-form point estimator. Define the outer bins explicitly (`-Inf` and `Inf`) for untrimmed specifications and verify every treated stratum has controls. Its simple plug-in variance conditions on estimated scores, cutpoints, and trimming; it is too optimistic as the primary interval. Use a stratified nonparametric bootstrap that refits the logit, reapplies support, and recomputes quintiles/weights in every replicate.\n\nThe full \\(2\\times2\\times2\\) grid is a good, transparent minimum: eight labeled specifications. Keep the logit functional form fixed across rows, and report post-design balance as well as retained counts. The naïve CPS contrast should be labeled descriptive, not causal.\n\nThe experimental benchmark as a horizontal line with its own table interval is fine; use a standard randomized difference-in-means interval and compute it from the data.\n\nDecision rule:\n\n- **Robust recovery:** estimates remain substantively close to the benchmark across both covariate sets, both estimators, and both support choices, with no systematic sensitivity and adequate balance/overlap.\n- **Favorable-specification-only recovery:** only a restricted subset—plausibly earnings-adjusted and/or overlap-trimmed rows—are close, while credible alternatives materially differ. This supports a conditional, design-dependent claim only.\n- **No recovery:** no defensible prespecified row is close, or estimates remain systematically far from the benchmark after adequate balance/support diagnostics.\n\nCrucially, closeness of an overlap-trimmed estimate to the full-sample experimental benchmark alone is not evidence of recovery of the full NSW-treated ATT.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
